{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_018h3CwtibEHF61z2HUpoG2M","type":"tool_result","content":"1\texport interface BlogPost {\n2\t  slug: string;\n3\t  title: string;\n4\t  excerpt: string;\n5\t  content: string; // HTML string\n6\t  date: string; // YYYY-MM-DD (published)\n7\t  lastModified?: string; // YYYY-MM-DD (last edited — used as dateModified in schema)\n8\t  category: string;\n9\t  author: string;\n10\t  readTime: string;\n11\t  readingTimeMinutes: number;\n12\t  tags: string[];\n13\t  featuredImage: string;\n14\t}\n15\t\n16\t/** Compute reading time from HTML content (200 wpm, minimum 1 min). */\n17\tfunction computeReadingTime(html: string): number {\n18\t  const text = html.replace(/<[^>]+>/g, \" \").replace(/\\s+/g, \" \").trim();\n19\t  const wordCount = text.split(\" \").filter(Boolean).length;\n20\t  return Math.max(1, Math.ceil(wordCount / 200));\n21\t}\n22\t\n23\t/** Converts a category label to a URL-safe slug. e.g. \"Food & Drink\" → \"food-drink\" */\n24\texport function categoryToSlug(category: string): string {\n25\t  return category\n26\t    .toLowerCase()\n27\t    .replace(/[^a-z0-9\\s]/g, \"\")\n28\t    .trim()\n29\t    .replace(/\\s+/g, \"-\");\n30\t}\n31\t\n32\t/** Converts a category slug back to a display label, or returns null if not found. */\n33\texport function slugToCategory(slug: string): string | null {\n34\t  const found = blogPosts.find((p) => categoryToSlug(p.category) === slug);\n35\t  return found ? found.category : null;\n36\t}\n37\t\n38\t/** All unique categories in the blog. */\n39\texport function getUniqueCategories(): string[] {\n40\t  return [...new Set(blogPosts.map((p) => p.category))];\n41\t}\n42\t\n43\tconst rawPosts: Omit<BlogPost, \"readingTimeMinutes\">[] = [\n44\t  {\n45\t    slug: \"ultimate-jb-food-guide-backpackers\",\n46\t    title: \"The Ultimate Johor Bahru Food Guide for Backpackers (2026)\",\n47\t    excerpt:\n48\t      \"From RM5 hawker meals to legendary seafood spots — everything a budget traveller needs to eat their way through JB like a local.\",\n49\t    date: \"2026-02-20\",\n50\t    category: \"Food & Drink\",\n51\t    author: \"Jay Lew\",\n52\t    readTime: \"8 min read\",\n53\t    tags: [\"johor-bahru\", \"food\", \"backpacker\", \"budget-travel\", \"malaysia\", \"hawker\"],\n54\t    featuredImage: \"/images/blog/jb-food-guide.jpg\",\n55\t    content: `\n56\t<h2>Why JB Is a Backpacker's Food Paradise</h2>\n57\t<p>Johor Bahru sits at the crossroads of Malay, Chinese, Indian, and Peranakan culinary traditions — and the prices are absurdly low. A full meal at a hawker centre runs RM5–12, a pulled tea costs RM2, and the quality of food rivals cities ten times its size. For backpackers crossing the border from Singapore, where a similar meal costs SGD8–15, JB feels like a food revelation.</p>\n58\t\n59\t<p>The city doesn't have Michelin stars or Instagram-bait restaurants. What it has is better: generations-old recipes, family-run stalls, and an eating culture where quality is the only way to survive. If you're staying at <a href=\"/rooms\">Pelangi Capsule Hostel in Taman Pelangi</a>, you're in one of the best food neighbourhoods in the city.</p>\n60\t\n61\t<h2>Breakfast: The Malaysian Way</h2>\n62\t<p><strong>Roti canai</strong> is the quintessential Malaysian breakfast — flaky, buttery flatbread torn by hand and dipped into dhal or fish curry. Every mamak (Indian-Muslim coffee shop) makes it, and it costs RM1.50–3. Pair it with a <strong>teh tarik</strong> (pulled milk tea, RM2) and you've got a breakfast that costs less than a Singapore MRT ride.</p>\n63\t\n64\t<p>If you want something heartier, try <strong>nasi lemak</strong> — coconut rice served with sambal chilli, fried anchovies, peanuts, cucumber, and a hard-boiled egg. The stall versions, wrapped in banana leaf, run RM3–5 and are available from dawn. For the adventurous, <strong>nasi kerabu</strong> (blue-coloured herbed rice) appears at Malay stalls and is worth seeking out.</p>\n65\t\n66\t<h2>Lunch: Hawker Centre Essentials</h2>\n67\t<p>JB's hawker centres are the backbone of the city's food culture. At lunch, every centre buzzes with office workers, families, and the occasional bewildered tourist who can't believe the prices. Head to <strong>Sik Bao Sin</strong> near Taman Pelangi for mixed rice — point at whatever looks good behind the glass, and the uncle will pile your plate for RM5–8.</p>\n68\t\n69\t<p>For noodle lovers, <strong>mee rebus</strong> is a Johorean specialty: yellow noodles in a thick, slightly sweet potato-based gravy, topped with boiled egg, tofu, and fried shallots. It's hearty, warming, and unlike anything you'll find elsewhere in Malaysia. <strong>Laksa Johor</strong> is even more distinctive — it uses spaghetti-like noodles instead of rice noodles, swimming in a rich fish-based curry. It's divisive, but locals are fiercely proud of it.</p>\n70\t\n71\t<p>Check our <a href=\"/guide\">JB Travel Guide</a> for a walking map of the best hawker centres within reach of the hostel.</p>\n72\t\n73\t<h2>Dinner: Seafood and Night Markets</h2>\n74\t<p><strong>San Low Seafood</strong>, a five-minute walk from Pelangi Capsule, is legendary among locals for its <strong>San Lou Bee Hoon</strong> — dry-fried vermicelli with prawns, egg, and a savoury soy glaze. The dish looks simple but the wok technique is masterful. Budget RM15–25 per person for a seafood dinner here, which is still a fraction of Singapore prices.</p>\n75\t\n76\t<p>If you prefer street food, JB's night markets (pasar malam) are unmissable. <strong>Taman Sentosa Night Market</strong> runs Thursday and Sunday nights and stretches for hundreds of metres: satay sticks (RM0.60 each), lok-lok (skewered meats dipped in boiling broth, RM1–2 per stick), martabak (stuffed pancakes), and fresh coconut water. Budget RM15–25 for a full night market feast.</p>\n77\t\n78\t<h2>Snacks and Drinks</h2>\n79\t<p><strong>Otak-otak</strong> — spiced fish paste wrapped in banana leaf and grilled over charcoal — is sold by roadside vendors for RM1–2 per piece. Buy five and eat them walking. <strong>Kuih</strong> (bite-sized Malay cakes and sweets) appear at morning markets and cost RM0.50–1 each. Try <strong>kuih lapis</strong> (rainbow layered cake) and <strong>ondeh-ondeh</strong> (pandan balls filled with palm sugar).</p>\n80\t\n81\t<p>For drinks, the <strong>teh tarik</strong> (pulled tea) is an art form. Watch the vendor pour boiling tea between two cups from arm's length, creating a frothy, perfectly mixed drink. <strong>Cham</strong> (coffee mixed with tea) is another local favourite. Fresh sugarcane juice costs RM3 and is the best remedy for JB's tropical heat.</p>\n82\t\n83\t<h2>Budget Breakdown</h2>\n84\t<p>Here's what a day of eating in JB actually costs:</p>\n85\t<ul>\n86\t  <li><strong>Breakfast:</strong> Roti canai + teh tarik — RM5</li>\n87\t  <li><strong>Lunch:</strong> Hawker centre mixed rice — RM8</li>\n88\t  <li><strong>Snack:</strong> Otak-otak + sugarcane juice — RM5</li>\n89\t  <li><strong>Dinner:</strong> Night market feast — RM20</li>\n90\t  <li><strong>Total:</strong> RM38 per day (approximately SGD11 or USD8)</li>\n91\t</ul>\n92\t\n93\t<p>That's three full meals, snacks, and drinks for less than the cost of one restaurant meal in Singapore. This is why backpackers who discover JB's food scene never want to leave.</p>\n94\t\n95\t<p>Ready to eat your way through JB? <a href=\"/book\">Book a capsule at Pelangi</a> and start your food adventure in one of the best eating neighbourhoods in the city.</p>\n96\t`,\n97\t  },\n98\t  {\n99\t    slug: \"singapore-to-jb-day-trip-causeway-guide\",\n100\t    title: \"Singapore to JB Day Trip Guide: How to Cross the Causeway (2026)\",\n101\t    excerpt:\n102\t      \"The complete step-by-step guide to crossing the Causeway from Singapore to Johor Bahru — transport options, immigration tips, costs, and what to do once you arrive.\",\n103\t    date: \"2026-02-25\",\n104\t    category: \"Travel Tips\",\n105\t    author: \"Jay Lew\",\n106\t    readTime: \"9 min read\",\n107\t    tags: [\"johor-bahru\", \"singapore\", \"causeway\", \"border-crossing\", \"backpacker\", \"budget-travel\"],\n108\t    featuredImage: \"/images/blog/causeway-guide.jpg\",\n109\t    content: `\n110\t<h2>Why Cross the Causeway?</h2>\n111\t<p>The Johor–Singapore Causeway is one of the busiest land border crossings in the world, connecting Singapore's Woodlands district to Johor Bahru, Malaysia. For travellers based in Singapore, JB offers dramatically cheaper food, shopping, and accommodation — making it the most popular day trip from the island. For backpackers, it's even more strategic: stay in JB at a fraction of Singapore prices and day-trip into Singapore whenever you want.</p>\n112\t\n113\t<p>The crossing itself is straightforward once you know the drill. This guide covers every option, common mistakes, and what to expect in 2026.</p>\n114\t\n115\t<h2>Option 1: Bus from Woodlands (Most Popular)</h2>\n116\t<p>The <strong>Causeway Link CW1</strong> and <strong>SBS Transit 170</strong> buses depart from Woodlands Checkpoint on the Singapore side. Here's the process:</p>\n117\t<ol>\n118\t  <li><strong>Get to Woodlands:</strong> Take the MRT to Woodlands or Kranji station. From Kranji, bus 160 goes directly to the checkpoint.</li>\n119\t  <li><strong>Clear Singapore immigration:</strong> Everyone exits the bus, walks through Singapore passport control, and re-boards.</li>\n120\t  <li><strong>Cross the Causeway:</strong> The bus drives across the 1km bridge. Takes 5 minutes without traffic.</li>\n121\t  <li><strong>Clear Malaysian immigration:</strong> Everyone exits again, clears Malaysian passport control at JB CIQ (Customs, Immigration & Quarantine).</li>\n122\t  <li><strong>Arrive in JB:</strong> You're now at JB Sentral, connected to City Square Mall and the city centre.</li>\n123\t</ol>\n124\t<p>Cost: SGD1.50–2 per way. The entire crossing takes 30–60 minutes on a good day, or 2–3 hours during peak periods (Friday evenings, public holidays, school holidays).</p>\n125\t\n126\t<h2>Option 2: Train (RTS Link — Coming Soon)</h2>\n127\t<p>The Johor Bahru–Singapore Rapid Transit System (RTS Link) has been in development and will eventually connect Bukit Chagar in JB to Woodlands North in Singapore. Until it's operational, the bus remains your best bet. Check KTMB for any shuttle train services that may be running between JB Sentral and Woodlands.</p>\n128\t\n129\t<h2>Option 3: Private Car or Grab</h2>\n130\t<p>You can take a Grab from Singapore to JB, but you'll need to exit the car at both immigration checkpoints and re-board. Some drivers won't do cross-border trips. A more reliable option is booking a private transfer — expect SGD40–60 for the car. For solo backpackers, the bus is significantly cheaper and barely slower.</p>\n131\t\n132\t<h2>Immigration Tips</h2>\n133\t<ul>\n134\t  <li><strong>Passport validity:</strong> Your passport must be valid for at least 6 months from the date of entry into Malaysia.</li>\n135\t  <li><strong>Malaysia Digital Arrival Card (MDAC):</strong> Since 2024, most nationalities must complete the MDAC online before arrival. Do this before you reach the border — it takes 5 minutes and is free.</li>\n136\t  <li><strong>Peak hours to avoid:</strong> Friday 5–9pm (Singapore to JB), Sunday 5–9pm (JB to Singapore). Weekday mornings are the smoothest.</li>\n137\t  <li><strong>Keep your departure card:</strong> Malaysian immigration staples a small card into your passport. Don't lose it — you need it when leaving Malaysia.</li>\n138\t</ul>\n139\t\n140\t<h2>What to Do Once You Arrive in JB</h2>\n141\t<p>JB CIQ drops you at <strong>JB Sentral</strong>, the city's main transport hub. From here:</p>\n142\t<ul>\n143\t  <li><strong>City Square Mall</strong> is connected directly — air-conditioned shopping, food court, and money changers.</li>\n144\t  <li><strong>Jalan Tan Hiok Nee</strong> (10 min walk) — heritage street with indie cafes, art galleries, and the best street murals in JB.</li>\n145\t  <li><strong>Taman Pelangi</strong> (10 min Grab ride, RM8) — the neighbourhood where <a href=\"/rooms\">Pelangi Capsule Hostel</a> is located, surrounded by hawker centres and local restaurants.</li>\n146\t</ul>\n147\t\n148\t<p>For a complete rundown of activities, attractions, and local tips, check our <a href=\"/guide\">JB Backpacker's Guide</a>.</p>\n149\t\n150\t<h2>Costs: Singapore vs JB</h2>\n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\n</system-reminder>\n"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"41046260-3a91-4d74-9c3d-56808142ec7b","uuid":"5a5af1dd-a4ea-442c-8ee4-ec1724300f83","timestamp":"2026-04-14T10:52:43.240Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"C:\\Users\\Jyue\\Documents\\1-projects\\Software Projects\\pelangi-website\\site\\src\\data\\blog\\posts.ts","content":"export interface BlogPost {\n  slug: string;\n  title: string;\n  excerpt: string;\n  content: string; // HTML string\n  date: string; // YYYY-MM-DD (published)\n  lastModified?: string; // YYYY-MM-DD (last edited — used as dateModified in schema)\n  category: string;\n  author: string;\n  readTime: string;\n  readingTimeMinutes: number;\n  tags: string[];\n  featuredImage: string;\n}\n\n/** Compute reading time from HTML content (200 wpm, minimum 1 min). */\nfunction computeReadingTime(html: string): number {\n  const text = html.replace(/<[^>]+>/g, \" \").replace(/\\s+/g, \" \").trim();\n  const wordCount = text.split(\" \").filter(Boolean).length;\n  return Math.max(1, Math.ceil(wordCount / 200));\n}\n\n/** Converts a category label to a URL-safe slug. e.g. \"Food & Drink\" → \"food-drink\" */\nexport function categoryToSlug(category: string): string {\n  return category\n    .toLowerCase()\n    .replace(/[^a-z0-9\\s]/g, \"\")\n    .trim()\n    .replace(/\\s+/g, \"-\");\n}\n\n/** Converts a category slug back to a display label, or returns null if not found. */\nexport function slugToCategory(slug: string): string | null {\n  const found = blogPosts.find((p) => categoryToSlug(p.category) === slug);\n  return found ? found.category : null;\n}\n\n/** All unique categories in the blog. */\nexport function getUniqueCategories(): string[] {\n  return [...new Set(blogPosts.map((p) => p.category))];\n}\n\nconst rawPosts: Omit<BlogPost, \"readingTimeMinutes\">[] = [\n  {\n    slug: \"ultimate-jb-food-guide-backpackers\",\n    title: \"The Ultimate Johor Bahru Food Guide for Backpackers (2026)\",\n    excerpt:\n      \"From RM5 hawker meals to legendary seafood spots — everything a budget traveller needs to eat their way through JB like a local.\",\n    date: \"2026-02-20\",\n    category: \"Food & Drink\",\n    author: \"Jay Lew\",\n    readTime: \"8 min read\",\n    tags: [\"johor-bahru\", \"food\", \"backpacker\", \"budget-travel\", \"malaysia\", \"hawker\"],\n    featuredImage: \"/images/blog/jb-food-guide.jpg\",\n    content: `\n<h2>Why JB Is a Backpacker's Food Paradise</h2>\n<p>Johor Bahru sits at the crossroads of Malay, Chinese, Indian, and Peranakan culinary traditions — and the prices are absurdly low. A full meal at a hawker centre runs RM5–12, a pulled tea costs RM2, and the quality of food rivals cities ten times its size. For backpackers crossing the border from Singapore, where a similar meal costs SGD8–15, JB feels like a food revelation.</p>\n\n<p>The city doesn't have Michelin stars or Instagram-bait restaurants. What it has is better: generations-old recipes, family-run stalls, and an eating culture where quality is the only way to survive. If you're staying at <a href=\"/rooms\">Pelangi Capsule Hostel in Taman Pelangi</a>, you're in one of the best food neighbourhoods in the city.</p>\n\n<h2>Breakfast: The Malaysian Way</h2>\n<p><strong>Roti canai</strong> is the quintessential Malaysian breakfast — flaky, buttery flatbread torn by hand and dipped into dhal or fish curry. Every mamak (Indian-Muslim coffee shop) makes it, and it costs RM1.50–3. Pair it with a <strong>teh tarik</strong> (pulled milk tea, RM2) and you've got a breakfast that costs less than a Singapore MRT ride.</p>\n\n<p>If you want something heartier, try <strong>nasi lemak</strong> — coconut rice served with sambal chilli, fried anchovies, peanuts, cucumber, and a hard-boiled egg. The stall versions, wrapped in banana leaf, run RM3–5 and are available from dawn. For the adventurous, <strong>nasi kerabu</strong> (blue-coloured herbed rice) appears at Malay stalls and is worth seeking out.</p>\n\n<h2>Lunch: Hawker Centre Essentials</h2>\n<p>JB's hawker centres are the backbone of the city's food culture. At lunch, every centre buzzes with office workers, families, and the occasional bewildered tourist who can't believe the prices. Head to <strong>Sik Bao Sin</strong> near Taman Pelangi for mixed rice — point at whatever looks good behind the glass, and the uncle will pile your plate for RM5–8.</p>\n\n<p>For noodle lovers, <strong>mee rebus</strong> is a Johorean specialty: yellow noodles in a thick, slightly sweet potato-based gravy, topped with boiled egg, tofu, and fried shallots. It's hearty, warming, and unlike anything you'll find elsewhere in Malaysia. <strong>Laksa Johor</strong> is even more distinctive — it uses spaghetti-like noodles instead of rice noodles, swimming in a rich fish-based curry. It's divisive, but locals are fiercely proud of it.</p>\n\n<p>Check our <a href=\"/guide\">JB Travel Guide</a> for a walking map of the best hawker centres within reach of the hostel.</p>\n\n<h2>Dinner: Seafood and Night Markets</h2>\n<p><strong>San Low Seafood</strong>, a five-minute walk from Pelangi Capsule, is legendary among locals for its <strong>San Lou Bee Hoon</strong> — dry-fried vermicelli with prawns, egg, and a savoury soy glaze. The dish looks simple but the wok technique is masterful. Budget RM15–25 per person for a seafood dinner here, which is still a fraction of Singapore prices.</p>\n\n<p>If you prefer street food, JB's night markets (pasar malam) are unmissable. <strong>Taman Sentosa Night Market</strong> runs Thursday and Sunday nights and stretches for hundreds of metres: satay sticks (RM0.60 each), lok-lok (skewered meats dipped in boiling broth, RM1–2 per stick), martabak (stuffed pancakes), and fresh coconut water. Budget RM15–25 for a full night market feast.</p>\n\n<h2>Snacks and Drinks</h2>\n<p><strong>Otak-otak</strong> — spiced fish paste wrapped in banana leaf and grilled over charcoal — is sold by roadside vendors for RM1–2 per piece. Buy five and eat them walking. <strong>Kuih</strong> (bite-sized Malay cakes and sweets) appear at morning markets and cost RM0.50–1 each. Try <strong>kuih lapis</strong> (rainbow layered cake) and <strong>ondeh-ondeh</strong> (pandan balls filled with palm sugar).</p>\n\n<p>For drinks, the <strong>teh tarik</strong> (pulled tea) is an art form. Watch the vendor pour boiling tea between two cups from arm's length, creating a frothy, perfectly mixed drink. <strong>Cham</strong> (coffee mixed with tea) is another local favourite. Fresh sugarcane juice costs RM3 and is the best remedy for JB's tropical heat.</p>\n\n<h2>Budget Breakdown</h2>\n<p>Here's what a day of eating in JB actually costs:</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>Breakfast:</strong> Roti canai + teh tarik — RM5</li>\n  <li><strong>Lunch:</strong> Hawker centre mixed rice — RM8</li>\n  <li><strong>Snack:</strong> Otak-otak + sugarcane juice — RM5</li>\n  <li><strong>Dinner:</strong> Night market feast — RM20</li>\n  <li><strong>Total:</strong> RM38 per day (approximately SGD11 or USD8)</li>\n</ul>\n\n<p>That's three full meals, snacks, and drinks for less than the cost of one restaurant meal in Singapore. This is why backpackers who discover JB's food scene never want to leave.</p>\n\n<p>Ready to eat your way through JB? <a href=\"/book\">Book a capsule at Pelangi</a> and start your food adventure in one of the best eating neighbourhoods in the city.</p>\n`,\n  },\n  {\n    slug: \"singapore-to-jb-day-trip-causeway-guide\",\n    title: \"Singapore to JB Day Trip Guide: How to Cross the Causeway (2026)\",\n    excerpt:\n      \"The complete step-by-step guide to crossing the Causeway from Singapore to Johor Bahru — transport options, immigration tips, costs, and what to do once you arrive.\",\n    date: \"2026-02-25\",\n    category: \"Travel Tips\",\n    author: \"Jay Lew\",\n    readTime: \"9 min read\",\n    tags: [\"johor-bahru\", \"singapore\", \"causeway\", \"border-crossing\", \"backpacker\", \"budget-travel\"],\n    featuredImage: \"/images/blog/causeway-guide.jpg\",\n    content: `\n<h2>Why Cross the Causeway?</h2>\n<p>The Johor–Singapore Causeway is one of the busiest land border crossings in the world, connecting Singapore's Woodlands district to Johor Bahru, Malaysia. For travellers based in Singapore, JB offers dramatically cheaper food, shopping, and accommodation — making it the most popular day trip from the island. For backpackers, it's even more strategic: stay in JB at a fraction of Singapore prices and day-trip into Singapore whenever you want.</p>\n\n<p>The crossing itself is straightforward once you know the drill. This guide covers every option, common mistakes, and what to expect in 2026.</p>\n\n<h2>Option 1: Bus from Woodlands (Most Popular)</h2>\n<p>The <strong>Causeway Link CW1</strong> and <strong>SBS Transit 170</strong> buses depart from Woodlands Checkpoint on the Singapore side. Here's the process:</p>\n<ol>\n  <li><strong>Get to Woodlands:</strong> Take the MRT to Woodlands or Kranji station. From Kranji, bus 160 goes directly to the checkpoint.</li>\n  <li><strong>Clear Singapore immigration:</strong> Everyone exits the bus, walks through Singapore passport control, and re-boards.</li>\n  <li><strong>Cross the Causeway:</strong> The bus drives across the 1km bridge. Takes 5 minutes without traffic.</li>\n  <li><strong>Clear Malaysian immigration:</strong> Everyone exits again, clears Malaysian passport control at JB CIQ (Customs, Immigration & Quarantine).</li>\n  <li><strong>Arrive in JB:</strong> You're now at JB Sentral, connected to City Square Mall and the city centre.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Cost: SGD1.50–2 per way. The entire crossing takes 30–60 minutes on a good day, or 2–3 hours during peak periods (Friday evenings, public holidays, school holidays).</p>\n\n<h2>Option 2: Train (RTS Link — Coming Soon)</h2>\n<p>The Johor Bahru–Singapore Rapid Transit System (RTS Link) has been in development and will eventually connect Bukit Chagar in JB to Woodlands North in Singapore. Until it's operational, the bus remains your best bet. Check KTMB for any shuttle train services that may be running between JB Sentral and Woodlands.</p>\n\n<h2>Option 3: Private Car or Grab</h2>\n<p>You can take a Grab from Singapore to JB, but you'll need to exit the car at both immigration checkpoints and re-board. Some drivers won't do cross-border trips. A more reliable option is booking a private transfer — expect SGD40–60 for the car. For solo backpackers, the bus is significantly cheaper and barely slower.</p>\n\n<h2>Immigration Tips</h2>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>Passport validity:</strong> Your passport must be valid for at least 6 months from the date of entry into Malaysia.</li>\n  <li><strong>Malaysia Digital Arrival Card (MDAC):</strong> Since 2024, most nationalities must complete the MDAC online before arrival. Do this before you reach the border — it takes 5 minutes and is free.</li>\n  <li><strong>Peak hours to avoid:</strong> Friday 5–9pm (Singapore to JB), Sunday 5–9pm (JB to Singapore). Weekday mornings are the smoothest.</li>\n  <li><strong>Keep your departure card:</strong> Malaysian immigration staples a small card into your passport. Don't lose it — you need it when leaving Malaysia.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h2>What to Do Once You Arrive in JB</h2>\n<p>JB CIQ drops you at <strong>JB Sentral</strong>, the city's main transport hub. From here:</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>City Square Mall</strong> is connected directly — air-conditioned shopping, food court, and money changers.</li>\n  <li><strong>Jalan Tan Hiok Nee</strong> (10 min walk) — heritage street with indie cafes, art galleries, and the best street murals in JB.</li>\n  <li><strong>Taman Pelangi</strong> (10 min Grab ride, RM8) — the neighbourhood where <a href=\"/rooms\">Pelangi Capsule Hostel</a> is located, surrounded by hawker centres and local restaurants.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<p>For a complete rundown of activities, attractions, and local tips, check our <a href=\"/guide\">JB Backpacker's Guide</a>.</p>\n\n<h2>Costs: Singapore vs JB</h2>","numLines":150,"startLine":1,"totalLines":1509}}}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-haiku-4-5-20251001","id":"msg_019gTJ5793djSauh3pb7M61D","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Good, now I understand the structure. Let me continue reading to see more examples and get a sense of where the posts are added.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":8,"cache_creation_input_tokens":4719,"cache_read_input_tokens":80595,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":4719},"output_tokens":1,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"41046260-3a91-4d74-9c3d-56808142ec7b","uuid":"1b1a4bbe-57ce-466c-bbf0-487b34cf011f"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-haiku-4-5-20251001","id":"msg_019gTJ5793djSauh3pb7M61D","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01SkV3sav4NXj2oTesv96kR8","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"C:\\Users\\Jyue\\Documents\\1-projects\\Software Projects\\pelangi-website\\site\\src\\data\\blog\\posts.ts","limit":200,"offset":600},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":8,"cache_creation_input_tokens":4719,"cache_read_input_tokens":80595,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":4719},"output_tokens":1,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"41046260-3a91-4d74-9c3d-56808142ec7b","uuid":"546ac0eb-51ee-44d7-823b-1357a83af9f8"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01SkV3sav4NXj2oTesv96kR8","type":"tool_result","content":"600\t<td>JB → Singapore downtown</td>\n601\t<td>RM8–12 / SGD4–5</td>\n602\t<td>35–50 min</td>\n603\t<td>Hourly</td>\n604\t</tr>\n605\t<tr>\n606\t<td><strong>Personal vehicle</strong></td>\n607\t<td>Johor Bahru → Singapore (own car)</td>\n608\t<td>RM30–50 (toll + petrol)</td>\n609\t<td>30–45 min</td>\n610\t<td>Own schedule</td>\n611\t</tr>\n612\t</table>\n613\t<p><strong>Pro tip:</strong> Causeway Link and SBS are cheapest and most frequent. Buy monthly passes for RM60–80 (saves 20% vs daily tickets). Causeway Link passes sold at JB Sentral; SBS through their Larkin Sentral office.</p>\n614\t\n615\t<h2>The CIQ Process: What to Expect</h2>\n616\t<p>CIQ stands for Customs, Immigration, Quarantine. You clear Malaysia's exit checkpoint, then Singapore's entry. On buses, everyone steps off, queues through their respective counters, and re-boards. The whole thing takes 20–40 minutes depending on how busy the checkpoint is.</p>\n617\t<p>Keep your passport and work pass accessible — not buried in your bag. Officers rarely stop regular commuters; the questions are standard (\"Where do you work?\" \"How long have you been commuting?\"). After a few weeks it becomes muscle memory.</p>\n618\t<p>The queue times vary significantly by hour. Before 7:30am and after 6pm are the most congested. If your work schedule has any flexibility, crossing mid-morning (9–11am) or mid-afternoon (2–4pm) can save 30 minutes each way — that's five hours a week compounded.</p>\n619\t\n620\t<h2>Monthly Budget: JB Living, Singapore Salary</h2>\n621\t<p>Here's a realistic monthly breakdown for a single commuter earning SGD5,500/month (typical Employment Pass salary):</p>\n622\t<table border=\"1\" cellpadding=\"8\" cellspacing=\"0\" style=\"width:100%; margin:16px 0; border-collapse:collapse;\">\n623\t<tr style=\"background:#f5f5f5;\">\n624\t<th><strong>Expense</strong></th>\n625\t<th><strong>Amount (SGD)</strong></th>\n626\t<th><strong>Amount (RM)</strong></th>\n627\t<th><strong>Notes</strong></th>\n628\t</tr>\n629\t<tr>\n630\t<td><strong>Rent (shared HDB, JB)</strong></td>\n631\t<td>SGD400–600</td>\n632\t<td>RM1,200–1,800</td>\n633\t<td>Budget shared room or low-cost private flat in Taman Pelangi area</td>\n634\t</tr>\n635\t<tr>\n636\t<td><strong>Transport (bus + local)</strong></td>\n637\t<td>SGD80–120</td>\n638\t<td>RM240–360</td>\n639\t<td>Monthly Causeway Link + local JB transport</td>\n640\t</tr>\n641\t<tr>\n642\t<td><strong>Food (breakfast + lunch + dinner)</strong></td>\n643\t<td>SGD250–350</td>\n644\t<td>RM750–1,050</td>\n645\t<td>Mix of hawker (RM5–8) and occasional restaurants</td>\n646\t</tr>\n647\t<tr>\n648\t<td><strong>Utilities (electric, water, WiFi)</strong></td>\n649\t<td>SGD40–60</td>\n650\t<td>RM120–180</td>\n651\t<td>Shared utilities in rented unit</td>\n652\t</tr>\n653\t<tr>\n654\t<td><strong>Phone + mobile data</strong></td>\n655\t<td>SGD15–25</td>\n656\t<td>RM45–75</td>\n657\t<td>Digi or Maxis unlimited plan</td>\n658\t</tr>\n659\t<tr>\n660\t<td><strong>Sundries (laundry, toiletries, misc)</strong></td>\n661\t<td>SGD50–80</td>\n662\t<td>RM150–240</td>\n663\t<td>Varies by lifestyle</td>\n664\t</tr>\n665\t<tr style=\"background:#f5f5f5;\"><td><strong>TOTAL MONTHLY</strong></td><td><strong>SGD835–1,235</strong></td><td><strong>RM2,505–3,705</strong></td><td><strong>Leaves SGD4,265–4,665 savings monthly</strong></td>\n666\t</tr>\n667\t</table>\n668\t<p>At this spending level, a SGD5,500 earner saves <strong>SGD50,000–56,000 annually</strong>. That funds a down payment, business startup, or career transition. Living in JB while earning Singapore salary is genuinely wealth-building.</p>\n669\t\n670\t<h2>Tips for Smooth Commuting</h2>\n671\t<ul>\n672\t<li><strong>Build in 15 minutes buffer.</strong> Delays happen — not every day, but often enough. Being late once or twice sets a bad tone early.</li>\n673\t<li><strong>Get the Causeway Link app.</strong> Real-time bus tracking, seat booking, and monthly pass management on iOS and Android. Saves time standing at the terminal.</li>\n674\t<li><strong>Join commuter Facebook groups.</strong> \"JB to Singapore Commuters\" has thousands of members sharing live traffic updates, carpool arrangements, and tips that don't appear in any guide.</li>\n675\t<li><strong>Keep your passport accessible.</strong> A small document pouch worn on your belt or tucked in a front pocket means you're through immigration faster than people digging through bags.</li>\n676\t<li><strong>Use the crossing time well.</strong> It's roughly 45 minutes each way. Commuters who read, listen to podcasts, or sleep during the trip find it much more tolerable than those who just stare at their phones.</li>\n677\t</ul>\n678\t\n679\t<h2>Where to Stay When You First Arrive</h2>\n680\t<p>For the first 1–3 months while you find a longer-term place, <a href=\"/rooms\">Pelangi Capsule Hostel</a> in Taman Pelangi works well as a base. Japanese-style capsule pods from RM35–50/night, hot showers, lockers, and fast WiFi. The neighbourhood has hawker centres, banks, and transport options nearby. It's also where a lot of other commuters and new arrivals stay, which is useful if you're navigating this for the first time.</p>\n681\t\n682\t<h2>Finding Longer-Term Housing</h2>\n683\t<p>Once your work pass is sorted, switch to a 12-month lease. Target neighbourhoods near JB Sentral and the Causeway: Taman Pelangi, Danga Bay, and Larkin are all reasonable. One-bedroom private flats in these areas run RM800–1,500/month. <a href=\"/getting-here\">Getting to Pelangi from the Causeway</a> takes 10–15 minutes by Grab, so it's a decent indicator of transport connections for any flat you're considering in the same area.</p>\n684\t\n685\t<h2>Is It Worth It?</h2>\n686\t<p>Daily commuting isn't glamorous. The Causeway is a routine, not an adventure. But most commuters settle into the rhythm within two weeks, and the financial upside — SGD50,000+ in savings annually at a mid-level salary — is hard to argue with. Weather delays are rare, roadwork is predictable, and the bus runs reliably. The 1.5-hour daily round trip is the trade. Most people find it's worth it.</p>\n687\t\n688\t<p><a href=\"/rooms\">Book a stay at Pelangi Capsule</a> as your first base in JB, and start your cross-border commute from there.</p>\n689\t`,\n690\t  },\n691\t  {\n692\t    slug: \"gig-jobs-malaysia-waiting-singapore-work-pass\",\n693\t    title: \"5 Gig Jobs for Malaysians Waiting for Singapore Work Approval\",\n694\t    excerpt:\n695\t      \"Stuck in the work pass waiting period? Make money while you wait. Foodpanda, Lalamove, Grab, and more—5 flexible gig economy jobs in Malaysia with realistic earnings.\",\n696\t    date: \"2026-04-14\",\n697\t    category: \"Career & Work\",\n698\t    author: \"Jay Lew\",\n699\t    readTime: \"9 min read\",\n700\t    tags: [\"gig-economy\", \"malaysia\", \"work\", \"freelance\", \"income\", \"job-seeker\"],\n701\t    featuredImage: \"/images/blog/gig-jobs.jpg\",\n702\t    content: `\n703\t<h2>Making Money While You Wait</h2>\n704\t<p>Singapore work pass approvals take 4–12 weeks. Pelangi guests often ask: how do I earn during this gap? The answer: Malaysia's gig economy. You can earn RM1,500–4,000/month with zero commitment. Here are 5 proven gig jobs for your transition.</p>\n705\t\n706\t<h2>1. Foodpanda Delivery Rider — RM1,800–3,500/month</h2>\n707\t<p><strong>What:</strong> Deliver food via motorbike, flexible shifts, work 4–12 hours anytime.</p>\n708\t<p><strong>Requirements:</strong> Malaysian IC, motorcycle license, own motorbike, Foodpanda app. Age 18+. Background check is instant (10 minutes).</p>\n709\t<p><strong>Earnings:</strong> RM5–8 per delivery + tips. Peak hours (lunch 11am–2pm, dinner 6–9pm): RM80–120/day. Part-time: RM1,500–2,500/month. Full-time: RM3,000–3,500/month.</p>\n710\t<p><strong>Pros:</strong> Instant signup, daily payouts, complete schedule control, stay active. Popular with expats in transition.</p>\n711\t<p><strong>Cons:</strong> Weather-dependent, traffic stress, no benefits, rating-sensitive, fatigue from long hours.</p>\n712\t\n713\t<h2>2. Lalamove Driver — RM2,000–3,800/month</h2>\n714\t<p><strong>What:</strong> Deliver parcels and documents across city, less frequent than food delivery but higher pay per trip.</p>\n715\t<p><strong>Requirements:</strong> Malaysian IC, driving license, own car or motorbike, insurance. Age 18+. Vetting takes 2–3 days.</p>\n716\t<p><strong>Earnings:</strong> RM8–15 per delivery (distance-based). Corporate deadlines (end-month): RM20–30 per delivery. Part-time: RM1,800–2,500/month. Full-time: RM3,000–3,800/month.</p>\n717\t<p><strong>Pros:</strong> Higher per-delivery rate, less traffic pressure (packages don't spoil), flexible shifts, same-day payout.</p>\n718\t<p><strong>Cons:</strong> Fewer orders than food delivery, vehicle damage liability, parking costs, less suitable for motorbikes.</p>\n719\t\n720\t<h2>3. Grab Ride or GrabFood — RM2,500–4,200/month</h2>\n721\t<p><strong>What:</strong> GrabCar (passenger rides) or GrabFood (delivery). Grab's bigger ecosystem = more orders.</p>\n722\t<p><strong>Requirements:</strong> Malaysian IC, driving license (5+ years for GrabCar), own vehicle, car insurance. Age 21+ for rides, 18+ for food.</p>\n723\t<p><strong>Earnings:</strong> GrabCar: RM3–25 per ride. GrabFood: RM6–12 per delivery. Average 10–15 rides/day = RM80–150/day. Surge pricing (Friday evening, weekends) = 1.5–2x multiplier. Part-time: RM2,000–3,000/month. Full-time: RM3,500–4,200/month.</p>\n724\t<p><strong>Pros:</strong> Strong brand trust, transparent pay, surge pricing rewards peak work, high volume keeps you busy.</p>\n725\t<p><strong>Cons:</strong> GrabCar needs 5-year license, passenger interaction risks (safety at night), fuel costs high, wear-and-tear on car, cancellations hurt earnings.</p>\n726\t\n727\t<h2>4. Part-Time F&B / Cafe Work — RM1,500–2,500/month</h2>\n728\t<p><strong>What:</strong> Barista, bartender, waiter, kitchen help. Flexible shift work, common for gap periods.</p>\n729\t<p><strong>Requirements:</strong> Work visa or Malaysian employment rights, punctuality, basic English for customer roles.</p>\n730\t<p><strong>Earnings:</strong> RM8–12/hour + tips. 3–4 shifts/week, 6–8 hours = RM1,500–2,500/month. High-end bars: RM15–20/hour + 10% service split.</p>\n731\t<p><strong>Pros:</strong> Predictable hourly pay, tips add 20–30%, meals included, social environment, structured schedule.</p>\n732\t<p><strong>Cons:</strong> Fixed shifts (less flexible), standing fatigue, early/late hours, customer service stress.</p>\n733\t\n734\t<h2>5. Online Freelancing — RM1,200–5,000+/month</h2>\n735\t<p><strong>What:</strong> Virtual assistant, content writing, social media, graphic design, coding, translation. Laptop-based, any time.</p>\n736\t<p><strong>Requirements:</strong> Laptop, stable internet, relevant skills, portfolio, Upwork/Fiverr/Freelancer account.</p>\n737\t<p><strong>Earnings:</strong> Entry (RM5–10/hr): RM1,200–2,000/month. Mid (RM15–30/hr): RM2,500–4,000/month. Expert (RM30–100/hr+): RM4,000–8,000+/month. Ramp-up takes 2–3 months as reviews build.</p>\n738\t<p><strong>Pros:</strong> Location-independent, work your pace, scale by reputation, no commute, leverage existing skills.</p>\n739\t<p><strong>Cons:</strong> Slow start, platform competition, time-zone issues with Western clients, payment delays (30-day holds), self-discipline needed.</p>\n740\t\n741\t<h2>Combining Gigs for Max Income</h2>\n742\t<p>Smart approach: deliver food 5–6 hours/day (RM400–600) + freelance 3–4 hours evenings (RM150–300) = RM550–900/day. Pair active gigs (delivery, rides) with remote work. During slow hours (2–5pm), do freelance projects.</p>\n743\t\n744\t<h2>Tax and Legal</h2>\n745\t<p>Gig income is generally taxable. Keep earnings records. Check your work visa terms—S Pass and Employment Pass typically allow gig work outside your primary job. Confirm with your employer before starting.</p>\n746\t\n747\t<h2>Pelangi Guests & Gap-Filling</h2>\n748\t<p>Many guests stay with us during that 4–12 week limbo. Those who pick up gig work report three wins: (1) funding relocation costs, (2) staying mentally active, (3) natural downtime when returning to Pelangi. Several guests deliver Foodpanda and work from our common areas.</p>\n749\t\n750\t<h2>Next Steps</h2>\n751\t<p>Choose a gig: Foodpanda for ease, freelancing for scalability, F&B for social connection. <a href=\"/rooms\">Book a stay at Pelangi Capsule</a> as your base while you earn and prepare for Singapore.</p>\n752\t`,\n753\t  },\n754\t  {\n755\t    slug: \"3-day-jb-backpacker-itinerary-rm250\",\n756\t    title: \"3-Day JB Backpacker Itinerary Under RM250 (2026)\",\n757\t    excerpt:\n758\t      \"A complete 3-day itinerary for JB on a tight budget: RM35/night hostel, RM25/day food, RM10/day transport. Day-by-day breakdown with best food spots and activities.\",\n759\t    date: \"2026-04-14\",\n760\t    category: \"Backpacker Tips\",\n761\t    author: \"Jay Lew\",\n762\t    readTime: \"8 min read\",\n763\t    tags: [\"johor-bahru\", \"backpacker\", \"budget-travel\", \"itinerary\", \"malaysia\", \"hostel\"],\n764\t    featuredImage: \"/images/blog/jb-backpacker-itinerary.jpg\",\n765\t    content: `\n766\t<h2>The RM250 Challenge: Is It Possible?</h2>\n767\t<p>Three days in Johor Bahru for under RM250 total? Yes. This includes accommodation, all meals, transport, and activities. Here's the day-by-day breakdown that proves it.</p>\n768\t\n769\t<h2>Day 1: Arrival & City Orientation</h2>\n770\t<p><strong>Morning (9am–12pm):</strong> Arrive at JB Sentral station. Walk or take local bus (RM2) to <a href=\"/book\">Pelangi Capsule Hostel</a>. Check in early (RM35/night), drop bags, freshen up. Cost: RM2 transport.</p>\n771\t<p><strong>Afternoon (1pm–5pm):</strong> Lunch at Sik Bao Sin hawker centre—mixed rice with 3 items, RM6. Walk around Taman Pelangi neighbourhood (free). Visit the traditional Chinese temple nearby (free entry). Coffee at local kopitiam, RM3.</p>\n772\t<p><strong>Evening (6pm–10pm):</strong> Dinner at Taman Sentosa night market—satay skewers (5 sticks, RM3), lok-lok (RM4), teh tarik (RM2). Return to hostel. Total Day 1: RM55.</p>\n773\t\n774\t<h2>Day 2: Food & Culture Deep Dive</h2>\n775\t<p><strong>Morning (8am–11am):</strong> Breakfast—roti canai + teh tarik at mamak, RM5. Grab to nearby Johor Art Gallery or street art walk (free/RM5 entry). Visit local market, RM3 (exploratory snacks).</p>\n776\t<p><strong>Afternoon (12pm–4pm):</strong> Lunch at laksa stall, RM7 (laksa Johor specialty). Rest at hostel or visit free museum. Explore the Waterfront area by foot (free). Snack—martabak + juice, RM5.</p>\n777\t<p><strong>Evening (6pm–10pm):</strong> Dinner at San Low Seafood restaurant—dry-fried bee hoon (small portion), RM12. Dessert: ice kacang at hawker, RM2. Chat with other backpackers at hostel common area (free). Total Day 2: RM59.</p>\n778\t\n779\t<h2>Day 3: Last Day Exploration & Departure</h2>\n780\t<p><strong>Morning (8am–12pm):</strong> Breakfast—nasi lemak from stall, RM4. Visit Sultan Abu Bakar Museum or local mosque (RM5 entry or free). Shopping at Jaya Jusco mall (window shopping free, maybe grab a snack). Coffee, RM2.</p>\n781\t<p><strong>Afternoon (1pm–4pm):</strong> Lunch—chicken rice or mixed rice, RM6. Relax at hostel or visit one last hawker spot. Pack and checkout by 4pm.</p>\n782\t<p><strong>Evening (5pm+):</strong> Budget buffer for transport to airport/station (RM10–15). Total Day 3: RM27.</p>\n783\t\n784\t<h2>The Budget Breakdown</h2>\n785\t<table border=\"1\" cellpadding=\"10\" cellspacing=\"0\" style=\"width:100%; margin:15px 0; border-collapse:collapse;\">\n786\t  <tr style=\"background-color:#f0f0f0;\">\n787\t    <th style=\"text-align:left; padding:10px;\">Category</th>\n788\t    <th style=\"text-align:center; padding:10px;\">Per Night/Day</th>\n789\t    <th style=\"text-align:center; padding:10px;\">3 Days Total</th>\n790\t  </tr>\n791\t  <tr>\n792\t    <td style=\"padding:10px;\"><strong>Accommodation</strong> (Pelangi Capsule Hostel)</td>\n793\t    <td style=\"text-align:center; padding:10px;\">RM35</td>\n794\t    <td style=\"text-align:center; padding:10px;\">RM105</td>\n795\t  </tr>\n796\t  <tr style=\"background-color:#fafafa;\">\n797\t    <td style=\"padding:10px;\"><strong>Food</strong> (Breakfast + Lunch + Dinner + Snacks)</td>\n798\t    <td style=\"text-align:center; padding:10px;\">RM25</td>\n799\t    <td style=\"text-align:center; padding:10px;\">RM75</td>\n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\n</system-reminder>\n"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"41046260-3a91-4d74-9c3d-56808142ec7b","uuid":"721c1861-3199-4ccd-811c-3876fb4da04f","timestamp":"2026-04-14T10:52:46.318Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"C:\\Users\\Jyue\\Documents\\1-projects\\Software Projects\\pelangi-website\\site\\src\\data\\blog\\posts.ts","content":"<td>JB → Singapore downtown</td>\n<td>RM8–12 / SGD4–5</td>\n<td>35–50 min</td>\n<td>Hourly</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Personal vehicle</strong></td>\n<td>Johor Bahru → Singapore (own car)</td>\n<td>RM30–50 (toll + petrol)</td>\n<td>30–45 min</td>\n<td>Own schedule</td>\n</tr>\n</table>\n<p><strong>Pro tip:</strong> Causeway Link and SBS are cheapest and most frequent. Buy monthly passes for RM60–80 (saves 20% vs daily tickets). Causeway Link passes sold at JB Sentral; SBS through their Larkin Sentral office.</p>\n\n<h2>The CIQ Process: What to Expect</h2>\n<p>CIQ stands for Customs, Immigration, Quarantine. You clear Malaysia's exit checkpoint, then Singapore's entry. On buses, everyone steps off, queues through their respective counters, and re-boards. The whole thing takes 20–40 minutes depending on how busy the checkpoint is.</p>\n<p>Keep your passport and work pass accessible — not buried in your bag. Officers rarely stop regular commuters; the questions are standard (\"Where do you work?\" \"How long have you been commuting?\"). After a few weeks it becomes muscle memory.</p>\n<p>The queue times vary significantly by hour. Before 7:30am and after 6pm are the most congested. If your work schedule has any flexibility, crossing mid-morning (9–11am) or mid-afternoon (2–4pm) can save 30 minutes each way — that's five hours a week compounded.</p>\n\n<h2>Monthly Budget: JB Living, Singapore Salary</h2>\n<p>Here's a realistic monthly breakdown for a single commuter earning SGD5,500/month (typical Employment Pass salary):</p>\n<table border=\"1\" cellpadding=\"8\" cellspacing=\"0\" style=\"width:100%; margin:16px 0; border-collapse:collapse;\">\n<tr style=\"background:#f5f5f5;\">\n<th><strong>Expense</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Amount (SGD)</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Amount (RM)</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Notes</strong></th>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Rent (shared HDB, JB)</strong></td>\n<td>SGD400–600</td>\n<td>RM1,200–1,800</td>\n<td>Budget shared room or low-cost private flat in Taman Pelangi area</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Transport (bus + local)</strong></td>\n<td>SGD80–120</td>\n<td>RM240–360</td>\n<td>Monthly Causeway Link + local JB transport</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Food (breakfast + lunch + dinner)</strong></td>\n<td>SGD250–350</td>\n<td>RM750–1,050</td>\n<td>Mix of hawker (RM5–8) and occasional restaurants</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Utilities (electric, water, WiFi)</strong></td>\n<td>SGD40–60</td>\n<td>RM120–180</td>\n<td>Shared utilities in rented unit</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Phone + mobile data</strong></td>\n<td>SGD15–25</td>\n<td>RM45–75</td>\n<td>Digi or Maxis unlimited plan</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Sundries (laundry, toiletries, misc)</strong></td>\n<td>SGD50–80</td>\n<td>RM150–240</td>\n<td>Varies by lifestyle</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"background:#f5f5f5;\"><td><strong>TOTAL MONTHLY</strong></td><td><strong>SGD835–1,235</strong></td><td><strong>RM2,505–3,705</strong></td><td><strong>Leaves SGD4,265–4,665 savings monthly</strong></td>\n</tr>\n</table>\n<p>At this spending level, a SGD5,500 earner saves <strong>SGD50,000–56,000 annually</strong>. That funds a down payment, business startup, or career transition. Living in JB while earning Singapore salary is genuinely wealth-building.</p>\n\n<h2>Tips for Smooth Commuting</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Build in 15 minutes buffer.</strong> Delays happen — not every day, but often enough. Being late once or twice sets a bad tone early.</li>\n<li><strong>Get the Causeway Link app.</strong> Real-time bus tracking, seat booking, and monthly pass management on iOS and Android. Saves time standing at the terminal.</li>\n<li><strong>Join commuter Facebook groups.</strong> \"JB to Singapore Commuters\" has thousands of members sharing live traffic updates, carpool arrangements, and tips that don't appear in any guide.</li>\n<li><strong>Keep your passport accessible.</strong> A small document pouch worn on your belt or tucked in a front pocket means you're through immigration faster than people digging through bags.</li>\n<li><strong>Use the crossing time well.</strong> It's roughly 45 minutes each way. Commuters who read, listen to podcasts, or sleep during the trip find it much more tolerable than those who just stare at their phones.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h2>Where to Stay When You First Arrive</h2>\n<p>For the first 1–3 months while you find a longer-term place, <a href=\"/rooms\">Pelangi Capsule Hostel</a> in Taman Pelangi works well as a base. Japanese-style capsule pods from RM35–50/night, hot showers, lockers, and fast WiFi. The neighbourhood has hawker centres, banks, and transport options nearby. It's also where a lot of other commuters and new arrivals stay, which is useful if you're navigating this for the first time.</p>\n\n<h2>Finding Longer-Term Housing</h2>\n<p>Once your work pass is sorted, switch to a 12-month lease. Target neighbourhoods near JB Sentral and the Causeway: Taman Pelangi, Danga Bay, and Larkin are all reasonable. One-bedroom private flats in these areas run RM800–1,500/month. <a href=\"/getting-here\">Getting to Pelangi from the Causeway</a> takes 10–15 minutes by Grab, so it's a decent indicator of transport connections for any flat you're considering in the same area.</p>\n\n<h2>Is It Worth It?</h2>\n<p>Daily commuting isn't glamorous. The Causeway is a routine, not an adventure. But most commuters settle into the rhythm within two weeks, and the financial upside — SGD50,000+ in savings annually at a mid-level salary — is hard to argue with. Weather delays are rare, roadwork is predictable, and the bus runs reliably. The 1.5-hour daily round trip is the trade. Most people find it's worth it.</p>\n\n<p><a href=\"/rooms\">Book a stay at Pelangi Capsule</a> as your first base in JB, and start your cross-border commute from there.</p>\n`,\n  },\n  {\n    slug: \"gig-jobs-malaysia-waiting-singapore-work-pass\",\n    title: \"5 Gig Jobs for Malaysians Waiting for Singapore Work Approval\",\n    excerpt:\n      \"Stuck in the work pass waiting period? Make money while you wait. Foodpanda, Lalamove, Grab, and more—5 flexible gig economy jobs in Malaysia with realistic earnings.\",\n    date: \"2026-04-14\",\n    category: \"Career & Work\",\n    author: \"Jay Lew\",\n    readTime: \"9 min read\",\n    tags: [\"gig-economy\", \"malaysia\", \"work\", \"freelance\", \"income\", \"job-seeker\"],\n    featuredImage: \"/images/blog/gig-jobs.jpg\",\n    content: `\n<h2>Making Money While You Wait</h2>\n<p>Singapore work pass approvals take 4–12 weeks. Pelangi guests often ask: how do I earn during this gap? The answer: Malaysia's gig economy. You can earn RM1,500–4,000/month with zero commitment. Here are 5 proven gig jobs for your transition.</p>\n\n<h2>1. Foodpanda Delivery Rider — RM1,800–3,500/month</h2>\n<p><strong>What:</strong> Deliver food via motorbike, flexible shifts, work 4–12 hours anytime.</p>\n<p><strong>Requirements:</strong> Malaysian IC, motorcycle license, own motorbike, Foodpanda app. Age 18+. Background check is instant (10 minutes).</p>\n<p><strong>Earnings:</strong> RM5–8 per delivery + tips. Peak hours (lunch 11am–2pm, dinner 6–9pm): RM80–120/day. Part-time: RM1,500–2,500/month. Full-time: RM3,000–3,500/month.</p>\n<p><strong>Pros:</strong> Instant signup, daily payouts, complete schedule control, stay active. Popular with expats in transition.</p>\n<p><strong>Cons:</strong> Weather-dependent, traffic stress, no benefits, rating-sensitive, fatigue from long hours.</p>\n\n<h2>2. Lalamove Driver — RM2,000–3,800/month</h2>\n<p><strong>What:</strong> Deliver parcels and documents across city, less frequent than food delivery but higher pay per trip.</p>\n<p><strong>Requirements:</strong> Malaysian IC, driving license, own car or motorbike, insurance. Age 18+. Vetting takes 2–3 days.</p>\n<p><strong>Earnings:</strong> RM8–15 per delivery (distance-based). Corporate deadlines (end-month): RM20–30 per delivery. Part-time: RM1,800–2,500/month. Full-time: RM3,000–3,800/month.</p>\n<p><strong>Pros:</strong> Higher per-delivery rate, less traffic pressure (packages don't spoil), flexible shifts, same-day payout.</p>\n<p><strong>Cons:</strong> Fewer orders than food delivery, vehicle damage liability, parking costs, less suitable for motorbikes.</p>\n\n<h2>3. Grab Ride or GrabFood — RM2,500–4,200/month</h2>\n<p><strong>What:</strong> GrabCar (passenger rides) or GrabFood (delivery). Grab's bigger ecosystem = more orders.</p>\n<p><strong>Requirements:</strong> Malaysian IC, driving license (5+ years for GrabCar), own vehicle, car insurance. Age 21+ for rides, 18+ for food.</p>\n<p><strong>Earnings:</strong> GrabCar: RM3–25 per ride. GrabFood: RM6–12 per delivery. Average 10–15 rides/day = RM80–150/day. Surge pricing (Friday evening, weekends) = 1.5–2x multiplier. Part-time: RM2,000–3,000/month. Full-time: RM3,500–4,200/month.</p>\n<p><strong>Pros:</strong> Strong brand trust, transparent pay, surge pricing rewards peak work, high volume keeps you busy.</p>\n<p><strong>Cons:</strong> GrabCar needs 5-year license, passenger interaction risks (safety at night), fuel costs high, wear-and-tear on car, cancellations hurt earnings.</p>\n\n<h2>4. Part-Time F&B / Cafe Work — RM1,500–2,500/month</h2>\n<p><strong>What:</strong> Barista, bartender, waiter, kitchen help. Flexible shift work, common for gap periods.</p>\n<p><strong>Requirements:</strong> Work visa or Malaysian employment rights, punctuality, basic English for customer roles.</p>\n<p><strong>Earnings:</strong> RM8–12/hour + tips. 3–4 shifts/week, 6–8 hours = RM1,500–2,500/month. High-end bars: RM15–20/hour + 10% service split.</p>\n<p><strong>Pros:</strong> Predictable hourly pay, tips add 20–30%, meals included, social environment, structured schedule.</p>\n<p><strong>Cons:</strong> Fixed shifts (less flexible), standing fatigue, early/late hours, customer service stress.</p>\n\n<h2>5. Online Freelancing — RM1,200–5,000+/month</h2>\n<p><strong>What:</strong> Virtual assistant, content writing, social media, graphic design, coding, translation. Laptop-based, any time.</p>\n<p><strong>Requirements:</strong> Laptop, stable internet, relevant skills, portfolio, Upwork/Fiverr/Freelancer account.</p>\n<p><strong>Earnings:</strong> Entry (RM5–10/hr): RM1,200–2,000/month. Mid (RM15–30/hr): RM2,500–4,000/month. Expert (RM30–100/hr+): RM4,000–8,000+/month. Ramp-up takes 2–3 months as reviews build.</p>\n<p><strong>Pros:</strong> Location-independent, work your pace, scale by reputation, no commute, leverage existing skills.</p>\n<p><strong>Cons:</strong> Slow start, platform competition, time-zone issues with Western clients, payment delays (30-day holds), self-discipline needed.</p>\n\n<h2>Combining Gigs for Max Income</h2>\n<p>Smart approach: deliver food 5–6 hours/day (RM400–600) + freelance 3–4 hours evenings (RM150–300) = RM550–900/day. Pair active gigs (delivery, rides) with remote work. During slow hours (2–5pm), do freelance projects.</p>\n\n<h2>Tax and Legal</h2>\n<p>Gig income is generally taxable. Keep earnings records. Check your work visa terms—S Pass and Employment Pass typically allow gig work outside your primary job. Confirm with your employer before starting.</p>\n\n<h2>Pelangi Guests & Gap-Filling</h2>\n<p>Many guests stay with us during that 4–12 week limbo. Those who pick up gig work report three wins: (1) funding relocation costs, (2) staying mentally active, (3) natural downtime when returning to Pelangi. Several guests deliver Foodpanda and work from our common areas.</p>\n\n<h2>Next Steps</h2>\n<p>Choose a gig: Foodpanda for ease, freelancing for scalability, F&B for social connection. <a href=\"/rooms\">Book a stay at Pelangi Capsule</a> as your base while you earn and prepare for Singapore.</p>\n`,\n  },\n  {\n    slug: \"3-day-jb-backpacker-itinerary-rm250\",\n    title: \"3-Day JB Backpacker Itinerary Under RM250 (2026)\",\n    excerpt:\n      \"A complete 3-day itinerary for JB on a tight budget: RM35/night hostel, RM25/day food, RM10/day transport. Day-by-day breakdown with best food spots and activities.\",\n    date: \"2026-04-14\",\n    category: \"Backpacker Tips\",\n    author: \"Jay Lew\",\n    readTime: \"8 min read\",\n    tags: [\"johor-bahru\", \"backpacker\", \"budget-travel\", \"itinerary\", \"malaysia\", \"hostel\"],\n    featuredImage: \"/images/blog/jb-backpacker-itinerary.jpg\",\n    content: `\n<h2>The RM250 Challenge: Is It Possible?</h2>\n<p>Three days in Johor Bahru for under RM250 total? Yes. This includes accommodation, all meals, transport, and activities. Here's the day-by-day breakdown that proves it.</p>\n\n<h2>Day 1: Arrival & City Orientation</h2>\n<p><strong>Morning (9am–12pm):</strong> Arrive at JB Sentral station. Walk or take local bus (RM2) to <a href=\"/book\">Pelangi Capsule Hostel</a>. Check in early (RM35/night), drop bags, freshen up. Cost: RM2 transport.</p>\n<p><strong>Afternoon (1pm–5pm):</strong> Lunch at Sik Bao Sin hawker centre—mixed rice with 3 items, RM6. Walk around Taman Pelangi neighbourhood (free). Visit the traditional Chinese temple nearby (free entry). Coffee at local kopitiam, RM3.</p>\n<p><strong>Evening (6pm–10pm):</strong> Dinner at Taman Sentosa night market—satay skewers (5 sticks, RM3), lok-lok (RM4), teh tarik (RM2). Return to hostel. Total Day 1: RM55.</p>\n\n<h2>Day 2: Food & Culture Deep Dive</h2>\n<p><strong>Morning (8am–11am):</strong> Breakfast—roti canai + teh tarik at mamak, RM5. Grab to nearby Johor Art Gallery or street art walk (free/RM5 entry). Visit local market, RM3 (exploratory snacks).</p>\n<p><strong>Afternoon (12pm–4pm):</strong> Lunch at laksa stall, RM7 (laksa Johor specialty). Rest at hostel or visit free museum. Explore the Waterfront area by foot (free). Snack—martabak + juice, RM5.</p>\n<p><strong>Evening (6pm–10pm):</strong> Dinner at San Low Seafood restaurant—dry-fried bee hoon (small portion), RM12. Dessert: ice kacang at hawker, RM2. Chat with other backpackers at hostel common area (free). Total Day 2: RM59.</p>\n\n<h2>Day 3: Last Day Exploration & Departure</h2>\n<p><strong>Morning (8am–12pm):</strong> Breakfast—nasi lemak from stall, RM4. Visit Sultan Abu Bakar Museum or local mosque (RM5 entry or free). Shopping at Jaya Jusco mall (window shopping free, maybe grab a snack). Coffee, RM2.</p>\n<p><strong>Afternoon (1pm–4pm):</strong> Lunch—chicken rice or mixed rice, RM6. Relax at hostel or visit one last hawker spot. Pack and checkout by 4pm.</p>\n<p><strong>Evening (5pm+):</strong> Budget buffer for transport to airport/station (RM10–15). Total Day 3: RM27.</p>\n\n<h2>The Budget Breakdown</h2>\n<table border=\"1\" cellpadding=\"10\" cellspacing=\"0\" style=\"width:100%; margin:15px 0; border-collapse:collapse;\">\n  <tr style=\"background-color:#f0f0f0;\">\n    <th style=\"text-align:left; padding:10px;\">Category</th>\n    <th style=\"text-align:center; padding:10px;\">Per Night/Day</th>\n    <th style=\"text-align:center; padding:10px;\">3 Days Total</th>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td style=\"padding:10px;\"><strong>Accommodation</strong> (Pelangi Capsule Hostel)</td>\n    <td style=\"text-align:center; padding:10px;\">RM35</td>\n    <td style=\"text-align:center; padding:10px;\">RM105</td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr style=\"background-color:#fafafa;\">\n    <td style=\"padding:10px;\"><strong>Food</strong> (Breakfast + Lunch + Dinner + Snacks)</td>\n    <td style=\"text-align:center; padding:10px;\">RM25</td>\n    <td style=\"text-align:center; padding:10px;\">RM75</td>","numLines":200,"startLine":600,"totalLines":1509}}}
